<RomanProgram schemaVersion="29" APTVersion="Version 2026.2.1  " xmlns="http://www.stsci.edu/Roman/APT">
    <ProgramInformation>
        <ProgramPhase>Approved</ProgramPhase>
        <Title>CAR171 - Ghosts and Scattered light</Title>
        <Abstract>The purpose of this CAR is to:
A ) Map the wings of the optical PSF: 
1) know how far from bright sources to point to achieve target background (operational) 
2) to provide a map of outer PSF for low surface brightness science users
3) test upstream mirrors for scattering (not probed as part of SCIPA or PFAC). Perform tests on both sides of the field to map out PSF scattering with field dependence, such as from the tertiary.

and 

B) Assess scattering of bright sources on the FPA and outer structure, use to validate ray-tracing models that enable predictive modeling. Features here include secondary mirror support legs, baffle edge scatter and the rogue path, aperture stop scatter, light shield scatter (equivalent of “Dragon’s breath”). These will be quantified as normalized detector irradiance (NDI).</Abstract>
        <ProgramID>1023</ProgramID>
        <StsciEditNumber>1</StsciEditNumber>
        <ProgramCategory>NASA</ProgramCategory>
        <ProgramCategorySubtype></ProgramCategorySubtype>
        <AllowRestricted>true</AllowRestricted>
        <IncludesCalibrations>false</IncludesCalibrations>
        <PureParallelProgram>false</PureParallelProgram>
        <Cycle>0</Cycle>
        <ObservingDescription>This CAR is dedicated to the study of optical ghosts and scattered light in Roman / WFI. There are two main objectives:

CAR171.1 - Map the wings of the optical PSF:
▪ 1) Know how far from bright sources to point to achieve target background (operational)
▪ 2) To provide a map of outer PSF for low surface brightness science users
▪ 3) Test upstream mirrors for scattering (not probed as part of SCIPA or PFAC). Perform tests on both sides of the field to map out PSF scattering with field dependence, such as from the tertiary.

CAR171.2 - Stray-light from sources outside the field of view:
o Assess scattering of bright sources on the FPA and outer structure, use to validate ray-tracing models that enable predictive modeling. Features here include secondary mirror support legs, baffle edge scatter and the rogue path, aperture stop scatter, light shield scatter (equivalent of “Dragon’s breath”). These will be quantified as normalized detector irradiance (NDI).

o NOTES:

- WARNING: All the observations in this APT require specific V3PA angles. There is a discrepancy between the internally calculated V3PA angles and the scheduling windows calculated by the APT. This is being investigated in the Roman HelpDesk INC0221379. - A.S.Borlaff - NASA/ARC 

▪ We have identified a reduced set of activities to perform as a risk reduction earlier in Commissioning. These will facilitate exposure time adjustment or possibly source selection.
▪ All target sources are stellar and selected in the CVZ to facilitate scheduling and APT implementation</ObservingDescription>
